From: BMC firmware
Subject: cmm: an interface with a name of 15 characters ends the daemon

For every link message, mc_update_table copies the name of the interface
into a buffer of IFNAMSIZ bytes and puts the end of the string after it.
The kernel sends the name with its end: 16 bytes for a name of 15
characters, so the end is written one byte past the buffer, onto the
stack guard, and the daemon ends with a segmentation fault at address 0
(__stack_chk_fail of musl) when the function returns.

strongSwan makes such an interface every time charon starts: it tries
"xfrmi-test-<number>", cut to 15 characters, to see whether the kernel
has XFRM interfaces. On the board the daemon died once in every boot
of an image with strongSwan, and would die with every restart of IPsec.

The name is cut to the buffer, and a message without a name is left.

---
--- a/cmm/src/module_mcast.c
+++ b/cmm/src/module_mcast.c
@@ -429,7 +429,12 @@
 	if(!ifi->ifi_change)
 		return;
 
+	if(!tb[IFLA_IFNAME])
+		return;
+
 	len = RTA_PAYLOAD(tb[IFLA_IFNAME]);
+	if(len >= IFNAMSIZ)
+		len = IFNAMSIZ - 1;
 	
 	memcpy( ifname, RTA_DATA(tb[IFLA_IFNAME]), len );
 	ifname[len] = '\0';
